From 322a9f87a60894c3cdfa996797edecb638ef9bb7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: BellSoft <info@bell-sw.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 20:00:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(vendordb): read the vendor advisories from the OSV
 archives

The advisories of the vendor distributions are no longer looked up in Trivy
DB. They are served from the OSV bulk archives (<base-url>/<ecosystem>/all.zip)
by the shared github.com/bell-sw/osv-internal module: the archive of an
ecosystem is downloaded on first use, cached under <cache-dir>/<ecosystem>/
with a compact index, refreshed after 24 hours and evaluated locally. Aqua's
database remains the default for every other distribution, so no trivy-db
change is needed.

pkg/vendordb holds the process-wide source configured from the flags (like
db.Init for Trivy DB) and the Trivy presentation of a finding: the CVE as
vulnerability id, the OSV record id as vendor id, the severity and CVSS
vector the vendor publishes and the record as data source URL.

New flags, available on every command that takes the DB flags:

  --vendor-url         base URL (http(s), file:// or a directory) for
                         every product; unset, each product has its own
                         default: the OSV bucket for the distributions,
                         the module's product base URL for the standalone
                         runtimes
  --vendor-feed        "<product>=<url>", the archive of one product
                         (repeatable; wins over the base)
  --vendor-strict      fail when an archive is unavailable

--skip-db-update and --offline-scan use the cached archives as they are;
--download-db-only fetches the archives with the database; trivy clean
--vuln-db removes them. The vendor source ids are accepted by
--vuln-severity-source.

The OS types, their os-release recognition and the severity source ids take
their values from osv-internal (distro.IDLinux / IDHardened), as do the
tests; the data source of a finding is named after its ecosystem family.

When the archive of a product cannot be obtained (download failure, HTTP
error, updates skipped with nothing cached) one warning per product says
that its packages were NOT checked against the vendor's advisories; the
packages stay in the report without such findings and the scan completes
with the usual exit code. --vendor-strict makes it a failure.
