-### Remove Databases
+### BellSoft OSV archives
+Advisories for BellSoft products (Alpaquita Linux, BellSoft Hardened Containers,
+standalone Liberica JDK and Liberica NIK) are not part of the vulnerability
+database. They are read from OSV bulk archives, fetched on demand into
+`<cache-dir>/<ecosystem>/` and refreshed after 24 hours. By default
+the archives of the two distributions come from
+`https://osv-vulnerabilities.storage.googleapis.com` and those of Liberica JDK
+and Liberica NIK from `https://packages.bell-sw.com/osv`.
+
+```
+$ trivy image --vendor-url https://mirror.example.com/osv bellsoft/alpaquita-linux-base:stream-musl
+```
+
+`--vendor-url` (`TRIVY_VENDOR_URL`) replaces both defaults with one
+`http(s)://` base URL, `file://` URL or directory containing
+`<ecosystem>/all.zip`; `--vendor-feed "<ecosystem>=<url>"` sets the
+location of a single ecosystem. `--skip-db-update` and `--offline-scan` use the
+cached archives without contacting the network; `--download-db-only` downloads
+the archives together with the vulnerability database, and
+`trivy clean --vuln-db` removes them.
+
+When the archive of a product cannot be obtained (download failure, HTTP
+error, or updates skipped with nothing cached), Trivy prints a warning naming
+the product and the source, reports its packages without BellSoft findings
+and completes the rest of the scan. `--vendor-strict`
+(`TRIVY_VENDOR_STRICT`) makes this an error instead.
+
+## Remove Databases
 
 `trivy clean` command removes caches and databases.
 You can select which cache component to remove:
diff --git a/docs/guide/coverage/os/alpaquita.md b/docs/guide/coverage/os/alpaquita.md
index d6f2deb..3618c63 100644
--- a/docs/guide/coverage/os/alpaquita.md
+++ b/docs/guide/coverage/os/alpaquita.md
@@ -25,6 +25,27 @@ rather than `/lib/apk/db/installed`. Trivy looks in both locations.
 BellSoft publishes its own security advisories in OSV format, and these are used
 when scanning Alpaquita for vulnerabilities.
 
+The advisories are not part of the Trivy database. Trivy downloads the OSV bulk
+archive of the ecosystem it needs (`<base-url>/Alpaquita/all.zip`,
+`<base-url>/BellSoft%20Hardened%20Containers/all.zip`) from the OSV bucket on
+first use, keeps it for 24 hours under `<cache-dir>/<ecosystem>/`
+together with a compact index, and evaluates the ranges locally. The source
+location can be changed with `--vendor-url` (`TRIVY_VENDOR_URL`,
+`db.vendor-url`); it accepts an `http(s)://` base URL, a `file://` URL or
+a directory, so an internal mirror or an air-gapped copy of the archives works
+too. `--vendor-feed "<ecosystem>=<url>"` overrides one
+ecosystem only. `--skip-db-update` and `--offline-scan` use the cached archive
+as is; `--download-db-only` fetches the archives along with the Trivy database.
+If the archive cannot be obtained, Trivy warns that the packages were not
+checked against BellSoft advisories and completes the scan without them;
+`--vendor-strict` turns the warning into an error.
+
+The vulnerability id is the CVE (BellSoft records keep it in `upstream`); the
+BellSoft advisory id (`BELL-CVE-...`) is reported as the vendor id, and the
+severity is derived from the CVSS vector BellSoft publishes. Details known to
+the Trivy database for the same CVE (NVD severity, description, CWE ids) are
+added as usual.
+
 Advisories are scoped to a release, taken from `VERSION_ID` in `/etc/os-release`:
 `stream` for the rolling release and `23` or `25` for the LTS branches. The libc
 flavor (`musl` or `glibc`) does not affect advisory matching.
